r the largest share of GDP, reflecting household spending on goods and services. Investment (I) includes business investments in capital goods, which are essential for future production. Government spending (G) encompasses expenditures on public services and infrastructure, while net exports (X - M) account for the trade balance, indicating the difference between what a country sells abroad and what it purchases from other nations.\n\nThe interpretation of GDP figures is vital for understanding economic conditions. A rising GDP indicates economic growth, suggesting increased production and consumption, which can lead to higher employment levels and improved living standards. Conversely, a declining GDP may signal economic recession, prompting concerns about unemployment and reduced consumer spending.\n\nHowever, it is essential to recognize the limitations of GDP as an economic indicator. While it provides a snapshot of economic activity, it does not account for income distribution, environmental sustainability, or the informal economy. Therefore, policymakers often complement GDP analysis with other indicators to gain a more holistic view of economic well-being.\n\nIn summary, GDP is a crucial measure of the economic performance of the United States, reflecting the country's economic health over time. Its calculation through various approaches, particularly the expenditure method, allows for a comprehensive understanding of economic dynamics. Despite its limitations, GDP remains a central focus for economic analysis and policy formulation, underscoring its significance in evaluating the overall performance of the U.S. economy.\",140\r\n438,UNITED STATES,NOMINAL GDP,Nominal GDP is a measure of the economic output of the United States in current dollars,87\r\n440,UNITED STATES,TABLE 3.8,Table 3.8 provides a detailed account of the nominal and real GDP data for the United States,87\r\n985,UNITED STATES,ALBERT LUCCHINO,Albert Lucchino collected data relevant to the dem